<quiz>
  <question type="aitext">
    <exampledescription>Find the missing current-limiting resistor in a circuit diagram and calculate a suitable value. Combines diagram reading with a calculation.</exampledescription>
    <name>
      <text>Electronics: LED indicator circuit</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><img src="@@PLUGINFILE@@/led_circuit.jpg" alt="Circuit diagram: a 9 V battery B1 and switch S1 feeding two parallel branches, one containing resistor R1 (470 ohms) and LED1, the other containing LED2" width="720" height="420" style="max-width:100%;height:auto"></p>
<p>The circuit diagram shows an indicator panel. Identify the design fault, explain what will happen when switch S1 is closed, and state how the fault should be corrected. Support your answer with a calculation where appropriate.</p>]]></text>
      <file name="led_circuit.jpg" path="/" encoding="base64"></file>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>LED2 is connected directly across the 9 V supply with no current-limiting resistor. An LED has a very low resistance once its forward voltage (2 V) is exceeded, so the current through LED2 will rise far above its 20 mA maximum, and it will overheat and fail (burn out) almost immediately. It may also drain or overload the battery. LED1 is correct: R1 limits its current to (9 − 2) / 470 ≈ 15 mA. The fix is to add a series resistor to LED2; a minimum of (9 − 2) / 0.02 = 350 Ω is needed, so another 470 Ω resistor (or the nearest standard value at or above 350 Ω, e.g. 390 Ω) should be used.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>10</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <idnumber>elec-led-indicator-circuit</idnumber>
    <responseformat>plain</responseformat>
    <responsefieldlines>8</responsefieldlines>
    <minwordlimit/>
    <maxwordlimit>250</maxwordlimit>
    <graderinfo format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Expected answer: LED2 has no current-limiting (series) resistor. When S1 closes, excessive current flows through LED2 and it burns out. Fix: add a series resistor of at least (9 − 2) / 0.02 = 350 Ω (e.g. 390 Ω or 470 Ω). LED1/R1 is correct (about 15 mA).</p>]]></text>
    </graderinfo>
    <responsetemplate format="html">
      <text/>
    </responsetemplate>
    <aiprompt>You cannot see the image the student was shown, so use this description of it: a circuit diagram titled "Indicator panel circuit". A 9 V battery B1 (positive at the top) feeds a top rail through a closed switch S1. Between the top and bottom rails there are two parallel branches. Branch 1: a 470 ohm resistor R1 in series with LED1. Branch 2: LED2 connected directly between the rails with no resistor. Both LEDs are drawn with the correct polarity (anode towards the positive rail). A note under the diagram says both are red LEDs with a forward voltage of 2 V and a maximum current of 20 mA. The fault is that LED2 has no current-limiting resistor; LED1 with R1 is correct (about 15 mA).
Check whether the student identifies that LED2 has no current-limiting (series) resistor. They should explain that when S1 is closed the current through LED2 will be far above its 20 mA rating, so it will overheat and burn out (it may also drain or overload the battery). They should state the correction: add a series resistor to LED2. A full answer includes a calculation such as R = (9 − 2) / 0.02 = 350 ohms minimum, choosing a standard value at or above this (e.g. 390 or 470 ohms). Do not credit claims that the LEDs are reversed or that R1 is the wrong value; check any calculation the student gives for arithmetic errors.
Provide constructive feedback explaining what the student got right or missed.</aiprompt>
    <markscheme>10 marks: identifies that LED2 has no current-limiting resistor, explains that excessive current will make it overheat/burn out, AND states the fix with a correct calculation (minimum about 350 ohms, choosing a suitable value such as 390 or 470 ohms).
7 marks: identifies the missing resistor on LED2, explains the consequence, and says a resistor should be added, but gives no calculation or an incorrect one.
4 marks: identifies the missing resistor on LED2 but gives no correct consequence or fix.
0 marks: does not identify the fault, names the wrong component, or claims a fault that is not present (e.g. LEDs reversed, R1 wrong).</markscheme>
    <model/>
    <spellcheck>0</spellcheck>
    <sampleresponse>
      <response>LED2 has no current-limiting resistor. When S1 closes it gets the full 9 V, so the current will be far more than 20 mA and the LED will burn out. It needs a series resistor: R = (9 - 2) / 0.02 = 350 ohms, so use a 390 or 470 ohm resistor, like R1 does for LED1.</response>
    </sampleresponse>
    <sampleresponse>
      <response>LED2 doesn't have a resistor in series so too much current will flow and it will blow. Add a resistor in series with LED2.</response>
    </sampleresponse>
    <sampleresponse>
      <response>There is no resistor on LED2.</response>
    </sampleresponse>
    <sampleresponse>
      <response>LED1 is the wrong way round so it won't light. R1 is also too big at 470 ohms, it should be 100 ohms.</response>
    </sampleresponse>
